      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184246",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers. If $m^{2}n^{2}$ is even, then $m$ is even or $n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$mis odd ornis odd$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nIf $m$ and $n$ are {{field-3}}, then\n$2a + 1$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}\nwhere {{field-6}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-7}} into the expression $m^{2}n^{2}$, we can show that $m^{2}n^{2} =$ {{field-8}}, where $c$ is {{field-9}}.\n\nTherefore, since $m^{2}n^{2}$ {{field-10}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $m^{2}n^{2}$ is {{field-11}}."
